[{:db/id "q-168602/field-1/252c73ce3e8f476b56f89e7e27ce5fabd465a7255a7f180b47f169203605f1f9" :answer/id #uuid "fa0cf1ec-c924-5648-b786-8759da2b35ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "xy^3-x+y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-168602/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "9206c057-5c30-5908-b130-64e08684e928" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-168602/field-1/252c73ce3e8f476b56f89e7e27ce5fabd465a7255a7f180b47f169203605f1f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-168602/field-1/252c73ce3e8f476b56f89e7e27ce5fabd465a7255a7f180b47f169203605f1f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-168602" :question/id #uuid "471f5b43-cf20-52a4-816d-05056195d422" :question/math-academy-id "q-168602" :question/problem "An object generates an electric field given by $F(x,y)=(a{y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j$ in a planar coordinate system, where $a$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nGiven that $F(x,y)$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n$\\text{div}\\,F=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $6xy$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x,y)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $+\\,C,$\nwhere $C$ is a constant-" :question/worked-solution "In part 1, we found that $a=1.$ So, \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=({y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j.\n$$\n\nThe gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={y}^{3}-1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3x{y}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int ({y}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{y}^{3}-x+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3x{y}^{2}+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3x{y}^{2}+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x{y}^{2}+{g}^{′}(y) & =3x{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =1 \\\\ g(y) & =y+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=y+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x{y}^{3}-x+y+C.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-168602/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "97730a2e-3d3e-5a2c-b366-7a3b1eafbe63"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-168602"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-168606/field-1/136b22b31b53ca9a2526d855ba5b7e64040f9e3c0b2716cff2935a7ec0a334db" :answer/id #uuid "d57550e3-5b42-5fab-8f6f-11ed746e39aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{52}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-168606/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b5ae4930-98b7-587c-b744-ba36308d179f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-168606/field-1/136b22b31b53ca9a2526d855ba5b7e64040f9e3c0b2716cff2935a7ec0a334db" :answer-field/choices ["q-168606/field-1/136b22b31b53ca9a2526d855ba5b7e64040f9e3c0b2716cff2935a7ec0a334db"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-168606" :question/id #uuid "dfb23bb9-8f0b-5ae1-9648-07fed38bd216" :question/math-academy-id "q-168606" :question/problem "An object generates an electric field given by $F(x,y)=(a{y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j$ in a planar coordinate system, where $a$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nGiven that $F(x,y)$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n$\\text{div}\\,F=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $6xy$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nFind $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x,y)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $xy^3-x+y$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $+\\,C,$\nwhere $C$ is a constant-\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/assets/642a546fc498dfb0f01c0729959f9f68b8dc2d142d966673f204eda910f19211.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            Consider the square $C$ with side length $8$ centered at $O,$ shown above. The flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{1}$ is given by\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}^{}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $y^3-1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}s.$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nFind the average value of the function $f(x,y)=x{y}^{3}-x+y$ inside the square defined in part 4.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}_{\\text{ave}}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0$\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/assets/6fd34e9070cd9555ba790e7300bb78b4d19f1b71b231e8a92f4e8f306c3d4623.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The region $D$ in the first quadrant is enclosed between two quarter-circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $3,$ as shown above. \nIn polar coordinates, the following integral can be written as\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $r^2\\cos(\\theta)+r^2\\sin(\\theta)$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the total charge $Q$ of a thin metal plate that occupies the region $D$ defined in part 6 and has charge density $\\rho (x,y)=x+y.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $Q=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the region $D$ represents a quarter-annulus enclosed between the circles of the radii $1$ and $3$ centered at the origin.\n\nThe total charge $Q$ of an object occupying the region $D$ with charge density $\\rho (x,y)=x+y$ is given by\n\n$$\nQ={∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn part 6, by transforming to polar coordinates, we found that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{r}^{3}]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0))\\cdot (9-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{26}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{52}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the total charge of the object is $Q=\\frac{52}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-168606/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60ac666d-7325-57a6-b4cb-b702765344c8"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-168606"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-168601/field-1/e2b4df88cb947e33635a83d072e781d146c6708996bb6bb8bc378a9075bbada1" :answer/id #uuid "d7079b7e-7006-5ae0-b1a5-561ded9bd575" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6xy" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-168601/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "26d2c75f-33a1-55f4-a64e-4ec3a4e9a37a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-168601/field-1/e2b4df88cb947e33635a83d072e781d146c6708996bb6bb8bc378a9075bbada1" :answer-field/choices ["q-168601/field-1/e2b4df88cb947e33635a83d072e781d146c6708996bb6bb8bc378a9075bbada1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-168601" :question/id #uuid "27a34945-3d5e-591b-b312-7a451d23daf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-168601" :question/problem "An object generates an electric field given by $F(x,y)=(a{y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j$ in a planar coordinate system, where $a$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nGiven that $F(x,y)$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n$\\text{div}\\,F=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the divergence of $F$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn part 1, we found that $a=1.$ So, \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=({y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\cdot F & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}-1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x{y}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =0+6xy \\\\  & =6xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-168601/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c18e198-9ea9-57dc-b9af-8c77f0da27cd"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-168601"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-168605/field-1/2695e176027e579d42f8bc4c5c6accbd5738c6c9152d72a2a37845fbc5be5fb9" :answer/id #uuid "a651bcd8-98fe-5baa-9690-6a09742d0356" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r^2\\cos(\\theta)+r^2\\sin(\\theta)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-168605/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "afa6af64-7001-557f-b9d7-5d8ee051796c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-168605/field-1/2695e176027e579d42f8bc4c5c6accbd5738c6c9152d72a2a37845fbc5be5fb9" :answer-field/choices ["q-168605/field-1/2695e176027e579d42f8bc4c5c6accbd5738c6c9152d72a2a37845fbc5be5fb9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-168605" :question/id #uuid "29de82ee-c9ee-5673-b1fc-4f060a92a759" :question/math-academy-id "q-168605" :question/problem "An object generates an electric field given by $F(x,y)=(a{y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j$ in a planar coordinate system, where $a$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nGiven that $F(x,y)$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n$\\text{div}\\,F=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $6xy$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nFind $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x,y)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $xy^3-x+y$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $+\\,C,$\nwhere $C$ is a constant-\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/assets/642a546fc498dfb0f01c0729959f9f68b8dc2d142d966673f204eda910f19211.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            Consider the square $C$ with side length $8$ centered at $O,$ shown above. The flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{1}$ is given by\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}^{}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $y^3-1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}s.$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nFind the average value of the function $f(x,y)=x{y}^{3}-x+y$ inside the square defined in part 4.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}_{\\text{ave}}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/assets/6fd34e9070cd9555ba790e7300bb78b4d19f1b71b231e8a92f4e8f306c3d4623.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The region $D$ in the first quadrant is enclosed between two quarter-circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $3,$ as shown above. \nIn polar coordinates, the following integral can be written as\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the disk enclosed between the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-168605/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f13c3a8-ba48-5299-b335-3752e8b92ef4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-168605"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-168604/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "7ef1be8e-7b6c-51a7-86c1-dc6f24419d26" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-168604/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "9fc9c2b7-afa1-506d-bff1-fa2453baa91f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-168604/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-168604/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-168604" :question/id #uuid "1b790b39-2d5a-502f-8a9f-500e59fc2b54" :question/math-academy-id "q-168604" :question/problem "An object generates an electric field given by $F(x,y)=(a{y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j$ in a planar coordinate system, where $a$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nGiven that $F(x,y)$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n$\\text{div}\\,F=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $6xy$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nFind $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x,y)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $xy^3-x+y$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $+\\,C,$\nwhere $C$ is a constant-\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/assets/642a546fc498dfb0f01c0729959f9f68b8dc2d142d966673f204eda910f19211.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            Consider the square $C$ with side length $8$ centered at $O,$ shown above. The flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{1}$ is given by\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}^{}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $y^3-1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}s.$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the average value of the function $f(x,y)=x{y}^{3}-x+y$ inside the square defined in part 4.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}_{\\text{ave}}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The interior of the square defined in the previous part can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y)\\,:\\,-4<x<4,\\,-4<y<4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(R)=8\\cdot 8=64.\n$$\n\nApplying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(R)}\\,{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}{∬}_{R}(x{y}^{3}-x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}(x{y}^{3}-x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{[\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{4}-xy+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{y=-4}^{y=4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}[(64x-4x+8)-(64x+4x+8)]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}(-8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{-4}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}(16-16) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-168604/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b43eea5b-9da3-5fe0-a28c-cc01aed3627a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-168604"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-168600/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "3640e961-2087-5dbc-888b-ece2017017af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-168600/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "f38f1e39-5f1c-5ba3-aa97-533d7c2e2a78" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-168600/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-168600/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-168600" :question/id #uuid "27df1d8e-6834-5e44-8deb-08daa1d8936f" :question/math-academy-id "q-168600" :question/problem "An object generates an electric field given by $F(x,y)=(a{y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j$ in a planar coordinate system, where $a$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCurrent part:\n\nGiven that $F(x,y)$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n>$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor our vector field $F,$ the domain is $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=a{y}^{3}-1,\\,Q=3x{y}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3a{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a{y}^{2} & =3{y}^{2} \\\\ 3a & =3 \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-168600/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8d73fd7-cdeb-59f1-a84c-aab6defb3aa3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-168600"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-168603/field-1/b613fc91b6c4a6e85ac99852e71568546c654eb8211149804f174ad50aa23eb6" :answer/id #uuid "34c117c1-08bd-5c89-95ee-4ff1d579f0c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y^3-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-168603/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b244c666-8283-5c23-9e57-75f005a0fe77" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-168603/field-1/b613fc91b6c4a6e85ac99852e71568546c654eb8211149804f174ad50aa23eb6" :answer-field/choices ["q-168603/field-1/b613fc91b6c4a6e85ac99852e71568546c654eb8211149804f174ad50aa23eb6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-168603" :question/id #uuid "506c20c3-e02c-551d-91b9-182a5911222c" :question/math-academy-id "q-168603" :question/problem "An object generates an electric field given by $F(x,y)=(a{y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j$ in a planar coordinate system, where $a$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nGiven that $F(x,y)$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n$\\text{div}\\,F=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $6xy$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nFind $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x,y)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $xy^3-x+y$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $+\\,C,$\nwhere $C$ is a constant-\n\nCurrent part:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/assets/642a546fc498dfb0f01c0729959f9f68b8dc2d142d966673f204eda910f19211.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            Consider the square $C$ with side length $8$ centered at $O,$ shown above. The flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{1}$ is given by\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}^{}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}s.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{1}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $x$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $n=i.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081812/assets/1939a274191b89f69e51a929654489ed9038df0313f6e47b15e0ff55ad813066.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(({y}^{3}-1)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}+1)\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}{y}^{3}-1\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-168603/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9c8ca56-5ea8-547c-80ab-6b1f1347447e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-168603"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
